22:03:02 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: It may help to treat this as a boundary issue rather than a question of whether the sister’s advice is “right” or “wrong.” She can acknowledge the good intention while making clear what she needs. Some practical approaches: - **Respond briefly instead of debating:** “Thanks, I’ll think about that.” “I appreciate the suggestion—we’re going to handle it our way.” She doesn’t need to justify every decision. - **Say directly when she wants support rather than advice:** “I mostly need someone to listen right now, not solutions.” “If I want advice, I’ll ask—I’d appreciate just being able to vent today.” - **Set limits on recurring topics:** If the advice is about parenting, money, home decisions, etc., she might say: “I know you care, but this topic has become stressful for me. Let’s not discuss it unless I bring it up.” - **Have her husband support the boundary.** Since it is his sister, he should ideally take an active role—not as a referee, but as a partner. He could say privately, “We know you mean well, but frequent advice can feel like criticism. Please let us ask when we want input.” Consistent support from him matters a lot. - **Reduce exposure if needed.** She can shorten calls, change the subject, avoid discussing vulnerable decisions, or take a break from one-on-one conversations if the pattern continues. - **Stay calm and consistent.** A warm but repeated line is often more effective than one big confrontation. She can be polite without accepting unsolicited guidance. The goal is not to make the sister stop caring or offering opinions entirely; it is to establish that advice is welcome only when it is invited.
