22:03:14 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It’s fair to say that you may have experienced certain women—including your sister—as overly persistent, critical, or advice-giving. That can be genuinely exhausting. But “women in general are naggy” turns a frustrating behavior by particular people into a claim about an entire gender. Plenty of men repeatedly offer unsolicited advice, criticize, micromanage, or push their preferences too; and plenty of women do not. Often what gets labeled “nagging” is also shaped by family roles, communication habits, anxiety, or who is expected to manage household and relationship matters. The useful issue is the behavior itself: repeated unwanted advice after someone has signaled they do not want it. Calling that out directly is clearer and fairer than making it gender-based: > “I know you mean well, but when you keep giving advice after we’ve said we’ll handle it, it feels pressuring rather than helpful.” That gives the person a concrete chance to change, without assuming the problem is something inherent to women.
